if you're above a certain age you know  that miami vice was once the coolest  show on tv whether you're old enough to  remember it or not keep watching to find  out what made it so cool  miami vice was unlike anything else on  the air when it premiered in 1984. the  series sprung from the mind of nbc  entertainment president brandon tardikov  after being inspired by mtv the most  revolutionary thing on 80s television at  the time was mtv the network which at  the time aired music videos 24 hours a  day tardikov wanted nbc to cash in on  the craze and while brainstorming ideas  one day he wrote down two simple words  mtv cops he sent along this tiny memo to  hill street blues writer and producer  anthony yurkovich yurkovich immediately  started to work on a project that could  fit tartakov's vague description  yurkovich had gotten the idea for a  movie about police officers in miami  working vice aka the beat for drugs and  prostitution he turned it into a double  episode length pilot script initially  titled gold coast speaking to time in  1985 yurkovich had this to say about the  idea even when i was on hill street  blues i was collecting information on  miami it seemed to be an interesting  socio-economic tide pool the incredible  number of refugees from central america  and cuba the already extensive  cuban-american community and on top of  all that the drug trade  don johnson has scored some notable  parts since spending six years starring  as sonny crockett on miami vice he  played the title character on the light  in tone cop drama nash bridges and later  had to turn as a police chief with dark  secrets on hbo's watchman but he'll  likely never move out of the large  shadow cast by his big miami vice gig so  it's baffling to think that nbc almost  went another way brandon tartikov pushed  hard to cast larry wilcox's sonny  crockett on miami vice at the time  wilcox was just wrapping up a long run  as a motorcycle riding highway patrolman  on chips wilcox auditioned and filmed  some fight-based test shots for miami  vice only for them to cast johnson  instead who was just coming off of five  unsold pilots at one point the show's  producers toyed with casting two major  movie stars of the time jeff bridges and  nick nolte  part of what made miami vice so  different from other police and crime  shows of the 1980s was its look and by  look we mean the unique grooming style  and fashion forward clothes of don  johnson's character sunny crockett i  mean  not exactly up my alley style and  persona wise  heaven knows i'm no box of candy he  wasn't clean shaven and he didn't rock  an era appropriate mustache however he  did sport the fuzzy beginnings of a  beard as if he hadn't had a chance to  shave in a while johnson talked to  rolling stone about the look saying the  stubble was born out of the character  because it was intimated that he had  been up partying with drug dealers for  two or three days at a time that was  sort of an unspoken thing which is why  he was always unshaven and looked like  he slept in his clothes as for those  clothes countless guys co-opted the  sunny crockett look a light-colored  blazer over a bright t-shirt as well as  shoes with no socks all of those choices  were just practical johnson added the  rolled up sleeves was a function of the  fact that i had to have a jacket to  cover the gun in the holster i just  stripped everything down to the bare  minimum i didn't wear socks because it  was too hot to wear damn socks after all  they were in miami  the popularity of miami vice peaked in  its second season for the year of 1985  to 86 it ranked number nine in the  nielsen ratings nbc naturally ordered up  a third season but starred don johnson  bulked at his salary the 35 000 per  episode he'd been making up to that  point was commensurate for an actor on a  new show not the mega star of an ultra  successful hit according to the  associated press johnson wanted to raise  to upwards of one hundred thousand  dollars per episode he feuded with his  bosses at universal television and  refused to report to work until he got  what he wanted executives called his  bluff filing a 10 million dollar breach  of contract lawsuit and leaking the news  that they were prepared to let johnson  leave the series they even had two  potential actor replacements lined up  saying elsewhere star mark harmon and  the rising tree williams shortly after  those aggressive actions the parties  worked out a mutually agreeable  arrangement johnson returned to the  miami vice set as a newly minted member  of tv's highest paid actors club  show creator anthony yurkovich  supervised the pilot and first five  episodes of miami vice then moved on to  make movies for universal pictures this  left the many day-to-day operations in  the hands of executive producer michael  mann the show was so young and  relatively undeveloped that man could  greatly dictate its direction  particularly its visual style and pastel  heavy color palette man sought to  express miami's uniquely bright and  eye-popping look on camera inspired by  memories of the city from a mid-70s  vacation as well as a random trip to a  paint store one time when he was looking  at color chips he shared this experience  with the los angeles times i was playing  around with the chips and i realized  three colors become thematic two colors  don't three colors you can actually  start telling a chromatic story you can  create a mood with three colors as a  result miami vice employed a strict hues  policy miami vice episode director bobby  roth told time there are certain colors  you're not allowed to shoot such as red  and brown michael knows how things are  going to look on camera a lot of it is  very basic stuff that has never been  applied to tv miami vice was to use the  language of then and now both cool and  hot it was so cool that its theme song  for a time became the hottest pop song  in the land the extended version of  miami vice theme written and performed  by jan hammer went to number one on the  billboard hot 100 in november 1985. even  though hammer never made it to the pop  chart again his composition is still one  of only five tv theme songs to ever sit  atop the top 40. as well as hammer's  theme song performed the first miami  vice soundtrack album did even better  released in 1985 the record included  hammer's theme song and other bits of  incidental music along with pop songs  prominently featured on the series  including glenn frey's you belong to the  city and phil collins in the air tonight  it spent 11 weeks at number one in the  billboard album chart and sold 4 million  copies ranking it among the most  successful tv tie-in albums of all time  there's an old saying about how  imitation is the sincerest form of  flattery which means the people who made  miami vice must have been very touched  when the airwaves were flooded with  rip-offs in 1985 a year after nbc  premiered miami vice abc answered with  the insiders which it claimed offered  contemporary music and a highly stylized  visual approach nicholas campbell and  stoney jackson starred as edgy crime  reporter nick fox and an ex-con turned  informant named mackie in true vice  style the pilot episode contained fancy  cars a visceral sequence depicting a  seedy crime and almost everybody dressed  in hip clothes the insiders was canceled  after 13 episodes and abc's other vice  clone hollywood beat only lasted 14.  miami vice also helped lead to one of  the most beloved and successful sitcoms  of all time older actress doris roberts  and selma diamond performed a bit during  an event announcing nbc's  1984-85 season and joked that they were  starring on a show called miami nice  that gave tartakov an idea a show about  retirement age women all living together  in miami that show which hit the network  in 1985 to ratings and critical success  was none other than the golden girls  when miami vice debuted in 1984 the real  city of miami wasn't as glamorous and  flashy as the show made it seem  according to the miami herald the city  was recovering from riots and struggling  to house a large number of haitian  refugees drug cartels were gaining power  and influence and violent crime was  dramatically rising but miami vice made  miami's run-down art deco architectural  style look cool and vital that's a big  part of what makes the city's south  beach nightlife district so attractive  to this day crews fixed up and painted  over the doled with time exteriors of  disused buildings providing miami with a  free makeover that was revitalizing and  restorative don johnson talked about  this makeover to rolling stone saying we  painted the facades of virtually every  building up and down collins avenue and  ocean avenue to match the color palettes  we had for the show the whole town kind  of reinvented itself in the image of a  television show all those reworked  buildings allowed new business in miami  to flourish turning south beach into a  tourist destination which initially  catered to big fans of miami vice  not only did crockett and tubs dress  cool they drove cool too since they  frequently were going undercover and had  access to sweet wheels that the mpd  seized from drug lords the cops of miami  vice got to drive some enviable  automobiles detective stance wytek drove  a 1963 ford thunderbird detective tubbs  had a 1963 cadillac deville and  detective crockett initially cruised  with a ferrari 365 gtb4 daytona spider  according to popular mechanics  crockett's car wasn't actually a ferrari  instead it was custom made for the show  assembled out of a 1980 corvette with  some ferrari parts this car garnered so  much attention from fans and car lovers  that it rubbed executives at ferrari the  wrong way ferrari north america sought  to divert attention away from a car that  wasn't commercially available so they  provided two brand new ferrari testers  to the miami vice production the  testerosas arrived painted black but  when the cars didn't look good in some  of the show's many nighttime scenes the  crew threw on some white paint  what did all those things that made  miami vice so fresh and original  actually cost they cost a lot in the  first season of the show producers spent  about 1.3 million dollars per episode  that's 30 percent more than what other  police series spent for an installment  at the time when adjusted to today's  current dollar value miami vice cost 3.1  million dollars an episode and that's  before don johnson's big pay raise a  substantial segment of the budget each  week went to music early in its run  miami vice soundtrack supervisors spent  about ten thousand dollars each week to  license pre-existing well-known pop and  rock songs by the likes of u2 tina  turner phil collins and roxymusic that  costs a lot more than just hiring studio  musicians to make a generic soft rock  score  miami vice faded into the collective  turquoise and pink tinted memory in 1989  after falling to number 64 in the annual  nielsen ratings no longer the white hot  show it was just a few years earlier by  the mid 2000s the franchise's good name  had switched from passe to nostalgia and  series executive producer michael mann  had evolved into an accomplished and  louded filmmaker all of this however  made for the perfect climate for a miami  vice revival on the big screen according  to kinetoscope the film's production was  anything but perfect going over budget  and enduring filming delays star jamie  foxx grew so disenchanted that he left  the film during a location shoot in the  dominican republic and refused to come  back for any overseas work this forced  man to rewrite the ending and abandon  plans to shoot in paraguay fox and man  really didn't get along but that wasn't  an isolated situation according to a  crew member michael dressed down  everybody and humiliated everybody  audiences likely used to campy ironic  movie versions of era definitive tv  shows seemingly weren't ready for a  gritty dark r-rated drug trafficking  thriller critics weren't either and  reviewed it tepidly at the time the 2006  film earned a middling 63 million  dollars at the domestic box office less  than half of the film's enormous 135  million dollar budget  if filmmakers or producers had any plans  to bring back miami vice in the wake of  the 2006 film version the movie's  lackluster performance likely buried  those plans in the ground but 2006 is a  millennium ago to hollywood and in the  2010s talks started happening on giving  miami vice another high profile shot in  2014 universal pictures actively  accepted pitches from screenwriters who  wanted to take a shot at launching a new  franchise of crockett and tubbs  headlined adventures those films  unfortunately never came but later that  year original series star don johnson  told rolling stone that there was quote  talk recently about doing new episodes  and that he had a few ideas on how to  pass the torch narratively speaking to a  new generation of vice cops in 2017  nearly three years later after those  negotiations allegedly took place  variety reported that movie star vin  diesel and producer chris morgan were  hard at work on a new miami vice for nbc  tv writer peter mcmanus who'd penned  episodes of the mist and satisfaction  was hired to write the pilot script but  that new take on miami vice never showed  up either check out one of our newest  videos right here plus even more looper  videos about your favorite movies and tv  shows are coming soon subscribe to our  youtube channel and hit the bell so you  don't miss a single one  [Music]  you

